# Built from the publisher's revised template example.
BOOK = 'Matthew'
TESTAMENT = 'New Testament'
GENRE = 'gospels'
SUBTITLE = 'The King, His Kingdom, and the Fulfillment of Prophecy'
SECTIONS = [(1, 'Book Setting & Program Identity', [('p', 'This section introduces you to the book by explaining who wrote it, who received it, and what was happening when it was written. It also shows you which of God’s programs the book belongs to — Israel’s prophetic program or the Body of Christ’s mystery program. Knowing the setting and the program identity helps you read the book in its proper context and understand how its teachings fit into the larger story of Scripture.'), ('p', 'Matthew is a Gospel written by <b>Matthew</b>, also called Levi, a former tax collector turned disciple (Matt. 9:9). It presents Jesus as <b>Israel’s promised King</b>, the Son of David (Matt. 1:1), fulfilling Old Testament prophecy (Matt. 1:22; 2:15; 2:23). The book is written to an Israelite audience, emphasizing the kingdom promised in the prophets (Matt. 4:17).'), ('p', 'Matthew belongs to <b>Israel’s Prophecy Program</b>, not the Body of Christ. Jesus ministers to “the lost sheep of the house of Israel” (Matt. 15:24), sends His disciples only to Israel (Matt. 10:5–6), and announces the kingdom promised to Israel’s prophets (Matt. 4:17; Isa. 9:6–7).')]),
(2, 'Narrative Flow of Matthew', [('p', 'This section gives you a simple walkthrough of how the book unfolds. Instead of diving into details right away, it shows the major movements in the book — how Matthew presents Christ, how His teaching develops, and how the narrative builds toward His death, resurrection, and kingdom proclamation. Seeing the flow of the book helps you understand the big picture before you study the individual passages.'), ('h3', 'Movement 1 — The King’s Arrival (Chs. 1–4)'), ('p', 'Jesus’ genealogy proves He is the Son of David (Matt. 1:1). His birth fulfills prophecy (Matt. 1:22–23). John prepares the way (Matt. 3:3). Jesus announces the kingdom (Matt. 4:17).'), ('h3', 'Movement 2 — The King’s Teaching (Chs. 5–7)'), ('p', 'The Sermon on the Mount reveals kingdom righteousness (Matt. 5–7).'), ('h3', 'Movement 3 — The King’s Power (Chs. 8–10)'), ('p', 'Jesus heals, casts out demons, and calms storms (Matt. 8–9). He sends the twelve to Israel (Matt. 10:5–6).'), ('h3', 'Movement 4 — The King Rejected (Chs. 11–13)'), ('p', 'Israel rejects Christ (Matt. 11:20–24). Jesus teaches kingdom parables (Matt. 13).'), ('h3', 'Movement 5 — The King’s Authority (Chs. 14–20)'), ('p', 'Miracles, teaching, and predictions of His death (Matt. 16:21; 20:19).'), ('h3', 'Movement 6 — The King’s Final Week (Chs. 21–25)'), ('p', 'Triumphal entry (Matt. 21:1–9). Rebukes of Israel’s leaders (Matt. 23). The Olivet Discourse (Matt. 24–25).'), ('h3', 'Movement 7 — The King’s Death and Resurrection (Chs. 26–28)'), ('p', 'Crucifixion (Matt. 27). Resurrection (Matt. 28:6). The Great Commission (Matt. 28:18–20).')]),
(3, 'Program Themes in Matthew', [('p', 'This section highlights the major ideas emphasized in the book — the big doctrinal themes that shape how Matthew should be understood within Israel’s prophetic program. These themes are not verse-by-verse explanations; they are the book’s main emphases, the repeated ideas God wants readers to carry forward. Seeing these themes together helps you understand the book’s message before you look at the individual truths in the tables.'), ('ol', ['<b>Jesus the King</b> — Son of David (Matt. 1:1).', '<b>Fulfillment of Prophecy</b> — “That it might be fulfilled” (Matt. 1:22; 2:15; 2:23).', '<b>The Kingdom of Heaven</b> — Announced by Christ (Matt. 4:17).', '<b>Israel’s Calling</b> — Jesus sent to Israel (Matt. 15:24).', '<b>Israel’s Rejection</b> — Woes to Israel’s leaders (Matt. 23).', '<b>The Coming Kingdom</b> — Christ returns in glory (Matt. 24:30).'])]),
(4, 'Christ in Matthew', [('p', 'This section gathers what the book teaches about Christ — who He is, what He has done, and how He is revealed as Israel’s King. These statements help you see the book’s Christ-centered focus before you study the doctrinal truths in detail. By collecting these verses together, you get a clear picture of how Matthew presents Christ as King, Messiah, Teacher, Judge, and Risen Lord.'), ('ol', ['<b>Son of David</b> — Jesus is the promised King (Matt. 1:1).', '<b>Emmanuel</b> — “God with us” (Matt. 1:23).', '<b>Teacher and Lawgiver</b> — Sermon on the Mount (Matt. 5–7).', '<b>Miracle Worker</b> — He heals all manner of sickness (Matt. 4:23).', '<b>Messiah</b> — Peter confesses Him as Christ (Matt. 16:16).', '<b>Judge of Nations</b> — Sheep and goats judgment (Matt. 25:31–46).', '<b>Risen Lord</b> — “He is risen” (Matt. 28:6).'])]),
(5, 'Israel, the Law, and the Covenants in Matthew', [('p', 'This section helps you see how Matthew speaks about Israel, the law, and the covenants without mixing programs. Matthew operates within Israel’s prophetic framework, fulfilling promises made to the nation. By gathering these points together, this section shows you how to read Matthew in its own setting — as an Israel-program book — while recognizing what it says (and does not say) about the Body of Christ.'), ('ul', ['Jesus ministers to Israel (Matt. 15:24).', 'The disciples are sent only to Israel (Matt. 10:5–6).', 'Jesus teaches the law’s true meaning (Matt. 5:17–20).', 'The kingdom is Israel’s promised kingdom (Matt. 4:17; Dan. 2:44).', 'The covenants are assumed throughout (Matt. 1:1; 2 Sam. 7:12–16).', 'The book does <b>not</b> address the Body of Christ’s heavenly calling.'])]),
(6, 'Israel-Program Distinctions', [('p', 'This section highlights the teachings in Matthew that belong uniquely to Israel’s prophetic program — truths rooted in prophecy, covenant promises, national identity, and God’s dealings with Israel. By gathering these together, this section helps you see how Matthew contributes to Israel’s prophetic story without borrowing from the Body-of-Christ program.'), ('ul', ['Jesus as Israel’s King (Matt. 1:1).', 'The kingdom offered to Israel (Matt. 4:17).', 'Miracles as kingdom signs (Matt. 11:4–5).', 'Israel’s rejection (Matt. 23:37–39).', 'The Olivet Discourse for Israel (Matt. 24–25).', 'The future restoration (Matt. 19:28).'])]),
(7, 'Truths Table: What Matthew Says', [('p', 'This table gathers the key statements, instructions, and teachings in Matthew and shows which ones belong to Israel’s prophetic program. Each row lists a truth from the book, explains what it says, and identifies its program identity based on the prophetic setting. This helps you see, at a glance, what Matthew teaches directly to Israel and keeps those truths separate from universal principles that apply more broadly.'), ('table', ['Category', 'Truth Revealed in Matthew', 'Program Identity', 'Scripture'], [['Jesus the King', 'Son of David', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Matt. 1:1'], ['Kingdom Offered', 'Repent, kingdom at hand', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Matt. 4:17'], ['Law Fulfilled', 'Jesus fulfills the law', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Matt. 5:17'], ['Miracles', 'Signs of the kingdom', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Matt. 11:4–5'], ['Israel’s Rejection', 'Woes to leaders', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Matt. 23'], ['Olivet Discourse', 'Israel’s future', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Matt. 24–25']], [20, 38, 26, 16])]),
(8, 'Universal Truths Table (All People, All Time)', [('p', 'This table gathers the truths in Matthew that apply to everyone, not just Israel. These are principles rooted in God’s character — things that hold true across all programs and all ages. By separating these universal truths from Israel’s prophetic instructions, this section helps you see what Matthew teaches all believers everywhere and what it teaches Israel in its own setting.'), ('table', ['Universal Truth', 'Description', 'Scripture'], [['Love Your Enemies', 'Foundational righteousness', 'Matt. 5:44'], ['Seek God First', 'God provides', 'Matt. 6:33'], ['Golden Rule', 'Do unto others', 'Matt. 7:12'], ['Faith Moves Mountains', 'God responds to faith', 'Matt. 17:20'], ['Forgive Others', 'God values forgiveness', 'Matt. 6:14–15'], ['Christ Is Risen', 'Central truth for all', 'Matt. 28:6']], [26, 50, 24])]),
(9, 'Program-Safe Application Grid', [('p', 'This section shows how to apply the teachings of Matthew without mixing programs. The applications come from Israel’s prophetic story and from universal truths that apply to everyone. By keeping the applications tied to what the book actually says — and not borrowing Israel-program commands for the Body of Christ — this grid helps you respond to Matthew in a way that is faithful to its setting, clear, and safe.'), ('ol', ['<b>Seek God First</b> — “Seek ye first the kingdom of God” (Matt. 6:33).', '<b>Love Your Enemies</b> — Foundational righteousness (Matt. 5:44).', '<b>Forgive Freely</b> — Forgive others (Matt. 6:14–15).', '<b>Trust Christ’s Authority</b> — All authority given to Him (Matt. 28:18).', '<b>Live with Integrity</b> — Let your yes be yes (Matt. 5:37).', '<b>Rest in Christ’s Care</b> — He knows your needs (Matt. 6:32).', '<b>Build on the Rock</b> — Hear and do Christ’s words (Matt. 7:24).'])]),
(10, 'Teaching Outline for Matthew', [('p', 'This outline gives you a teacher-ready structure for presenting the book. It follows the narrative movements and highlights the major themes, making it easy to teach Matthew in classes, small groups, or personal study.'), ('olr', ['The King’s Arrival (Matt. 1–4)', 'The King’s Teaching (Matt. 5–7)', 'The King’s Power (Matt. 8–10)', 'The King Rejected (Matt. 11–13)', 'The King’s Authority (Matt. 14–20)', 'The King’s Final Week (Matt. 21–25)', 'The King’s Death and Resurrection (Matt. 26–28)'])]),
(11, 'Companion Usage and Study Suggestions', [('h3', 'How to Use This Companion in Personal Study'), ('p', 'This Companion is designed to walk alongside you as you read Matthew, helping you see both the prophetic structure of the book and the timeless truths rooted in God’s character. Use it slowly, chapter by chapter, letting the narrative movements, themes, and universal truths deepen your understanding. The Program-Safe Applications will guide your heart toward mercy, love of neighbour, and living by every word of God without confusing Israel’s covenant commands with the Body of Christ’s calling today.'), ('h3', 'How to Use This Companion in Small Groups or Classes'), ('p', 'This Companion provides a clear, structured framework that makes teaching Matthew simple and accessible. Each section gives you ready-to-use material: narrative movements for overview, prophecy-program themes for doctrinal clarity, universal truths for discussion, and a pastoral teaching outline for weekly lessons. Use the tables to help your group distinguish Israel’s covenant identity from universal principles, and use the Program-Safe Application Grid to foster meaningful, Christ-centered conversation.'), ('h3', 'How This Companion Fits Within the Acts Overlap Theology Series'), ('p', 'This Companion is part of a larger series designed to help believers read every book of Scripture without blurring God’s prophetic program for Israel and His mystery program for the Body of Christ. Within the Acts Overlap Theology Series, Matthew stands firmly in Israel’s covenant story, yet it still reveals universal truths that shape Christian character today. This Companion shows you how to honor the book’s prophetic context while drawing out the timeless principles that apply to all believers in every age.')])]

